  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=2$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(2,{135}^{∘}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
